.nh
.TH "RAIL0" "1" "Aug 2026" "rail0 CLI" ""

.SH NAME
rail0-payments-transaction - Fetch one of a payment's transactions


.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBrail0 payments transaction   [flags]\fP


.SH DESCRIPTION
Fetch a single transaction of a payment by its id — the id every lifecycle
command prints when the gateway accepts an operation (202).

.PP
The direct lookup for "what happened to THAT attempt": its status, the decoded
revert reason of a failed one, and whether a stuck one is 'redrivable'.
Readable by either participant.


.SH OPTIONS
\fB-h\fP, \fB--help\fP[=false]
	help for transaction


.SH OPTIONS INHERITED FROM PARENT COMMANDS
\fB--agent\fP[=false]
	Agent mode: one JSON envelope on stdout, JSON events on stderr, no prompts (env: RAIL0_AGENT=1)

.PP
\fB--base-url\fP=""
	RAIL0 gateway base URL (env: RAIL0_BASE_URL)

.PP
\fB--debug\fP[=false]
	Print HTTP request/response details to stderr

.PP
\fB--json\fP[=false]
	Force plain (uncolored) JSON output (useful in scripts)

.PP
\fB--pretty\fP[=false]
	Use the key-value/table view instead of JSON (terminal only)

.PP
\fB-q\fP, \fB--quiet\fP[=false]
	Print only the result's id/hash, one per line (useful in scripts)


.SH EXAMPLE
.EX
  rail0 payments transaction 0xabc123… 7f1c0c8e-…
.EE


.SH SEE ALSO
\fBrail0-payments(1)\fP
